{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And a soul, when it offers.</b> 'נֶפֶשׁ' is not said of any freewill offerings except the meal-offering — whose way is it to volunteer a meal-offering? A poor person's. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: I account it for him as if he offered his soul (Menachot 104)." "<b>Fine flour shall be his offering.</b> One who says 'it is upon me to bring a meal-offering' unspecified, brings a meal-offering of fine flour — for it is the first among the meal-offerings, and it is scooped while it is still fine flour, as is explained in the subject. Because five kinds of meal-offering are stated here, and all of them come baked before scooping except this one — therefore it is called 'meal-offering of fine flour.'" "<b>Fine flour.</b> 'סֹלֶת' is only from wheat, as it says ״סֹלֶת חִטִּים״ (Exodus 29). And a meal-offering is no less than a tenth, as it says ״וְעִשְּׂרֹן סֹלֶת … לְמִנְחָה״ (Leviticus 14) — a tenth for every meal-offering." "<b>And he shall pour oil on it.</b> On all of it." "<b>And he shall put frankincense on it.</b> On part of it — he places the frankincense to one side. And what do you see to say so? That a plural after a plural in the Torah serves only to restrict. Alternatively: oil — on all of it, because it is mixed with it and scooped with it, as it says ״מִסָּלְתָּהּ וּמִשַּׁמְנָהּ״; and frankincense — on part of it, because it is not mixed with it and not scooped with it, as it says ״עַל כָּל לְבֹנָתָהּ״ — that after he scoops, he gathers all the frankincense from upon it and burns it (Sifra)." "<b>And he shall pour, and he shall place, and he shall bring.</b> This teaches that pouring and mixing are valid in a service vessel (there)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:04:47.732516336Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}